  "id": "Compound::DB00424 - Gene::1132",
  "verbalization": "Hyoscyamine, through its interaction with Atropine, appears to exert a pharmacological influence on Chrm4, a receptor involved in various physiological processes. The evidence suggests that Hyoscyamine causes several adverse effects, all of which are mediated by Atropine, which binds to Chrm4. The lowest common ancestor of these relationships is (S)-Atropine, a specific enantiomer of Atropine, and the shared classification as an Antimuscarinic Agent underscores the pharmacological context of these interactions. The consistent involvement of Atropine in the causal chain, along with the binding of Atropine to Chrm4, indicates a potential mechanism by which Hyoscyamine may influence Chrm4 through Atropine. This pathway highlights the role of Atropine as a bridge between Hyoscyamine and Chrm4, emphasizing the importance of the Antimuscarinic Agent in mediating these effects. The integration of these relationships suggests a broader pharmacological mechanism where Hyoscyamine's effects are indirectly linked to Chrm4 via Atropine, reinforcing the significance of this interaction in the context of drug-target relationships.",
